That’s a useful persistence rule, with one correction: you control whether you quit, but you don’t control every ending.

A better version is:

The game is not over just because you’re losing. It’s over when reality closes the option set or you deliberately choose to stop.

And giving up is not the only way to lose. You can also lose by refusing to adapt, chasing a dead objective, or spending more than the outcome is worth.

So Pantheon’s version should be:

Persist in the mission, not necessarily the method.
Re-route before you surrender.
Change the game before you abandon the goal.
And know the difference between endurance and entrapment.

That preserves the spine without turning persistence into a sunk-cost trap.